<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
  Mission bring-up at login. THE HALF bringup.sh DOES NOT HAVE.

  bringup.sh answers "what should be running, and is it?". Nothing answers
  "who asks that question after a restart?". On 2026-08-17 the answer was
  "a human, forty minutes later, and only because someone thought to look" —
  ATOM-3 was still missing, `ok-1` had been writing to the shared git index
  for eleven minutes, and the 7-minute cycle was gone.

  WHY launchd AND NOT CRON. The cron used for the autonomous cycle is
  CronCreate, whose jobs are session-only and in-memory — the tool's own
  contract says they are gone when the Claude session ends. A restart
  therefore CANNOT be survived by that mechanism, by design, and job
  1f972d10 was never going to come back. launchd RunAtLoad is the platform's
  own answer and needs no daemon of ours.

  INSTALL (one command, not run for you — it persists across reboots):
    cp com.kingfisher.bringup.plist ~/Library/LaunchAgents/
    launchctl load -w ~/Library/LaunchAgents/com.kingfisher.bringup.plist

  RUN IT NOW WITHOUT REBOOTING:
    launchctl start com.kingfisher.bringup

  REMOVE:
    launchctl unload -w ~/Library/LaunchAgents/com.kingfisher.bringup.plist

  INVOKED VIA /bin/bash, NOT /bin/sh, AND THAT IS LOAD-BEARING. bringup.sh
  carries a `#!/bin/sh` shebang but uses bash arrays and process substitution
  (`done < <(...)`, line 35). Under /bin/sh it dies with "syntax error near
  unexpected token `<'" -- which happened on the first launchd run of this
  agent, into bringup.log, while the same script run by hand from an
  interactive bash worked perfectly. A harness that works in your shell and
  fails under launchd is almost always this or PATH.

  PATH IS SET EXPLICITLY AND THAT IS NOT OPTIONAL. launchd hands a job a
  minimal PATH — typically /usr/bin:/bin:/usr/sbin:/sbin — which does not
  contain /opt/homebrew/bin, where `claude` and `python3` live on this
  machine. A LaunchAgent that works by hand and fails at login is almost
  always this, and it fails silently into StandardErrorPath.

  THIS LAUNCHES AUTONOMOUS LANES AT EVERY LOGIN. bringup.sh's default mode
  starts every roster lane that is down; they run with
  --dangerously-skip-permissions against the shared git index. If you would
  rather a restart produce only a written census and leave starting to a
  human, add <string>--check</string> after the script path.

  bringup.sh's flag interface has changed twice while this file was being
  written (--start, then --check). If a login produces no lanes, read
  bringup.log first and check the flag still means what this comment says.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
  <key>Label</key>
  <string>com.kingfisher.bringup</string>

  <key>ProgramArguments</key>
  <array>
    <string>/bin/bash</string>
    <string>/Users/victorianikolenko/kingfisher/bringup.sh</string>
  </array>

  <key>WorkingDirectory</key>
  <string>/Users/victorianikolenko/kingfisher</string>

  <key>EnvironmentVariables</key>
  <dict>
    <key>PATH</key>
    <string>/opt/homebrew/bin:/usr/bin:/bin:/usr/sbin:/sbin</string>
    <key>HOME</key>
    <string>/Users/victorianikolenko</string>
  </dict>

  <key>RunAtLoad</key>
  <true/>

  <!-- Re-reconcile every 10 minutes, not only at login. Adopted from the
       net.kingfisher.bringup agent another lane installed at 13:42, which had
       this and was merged into this file. It replaces the CronCreate cycle that
       died with the restart and, by that tool's own contract, could never have
       survived one: those jobs are session-only and in-memory. A lane that dies
       mid-day is now picked up within 10 minutes instead of at next login. -->
  <key>StartInterval</key>
  <integer>600</integer>

  <!-- Not KeepAlive. bringup.sh is a reconciler that exits; KeepAlive would
       respawn it in a tight loop forever. run_loop.sh is the thing that keeps
       a lane alive, and it already self-detaches and backs off. -->
  <key>KeepAlive</key>
  <false/>

  <!-- H179, 2026-08-19 (ok-1). `man launchd.plist`: "When a job dies, launchd
       kills any remaining processes with the same process group ID as the job.
       Setting this key to true disables that behavior." This key was ABSENT, so
       it defaulted to false, and bringup.sh's `CALLSIGN=$lane ./run_loop.sh &`
       leaves each lane in this job's process group -- the double-fork detach
       changes the lane's PARENT, never its GROUP. So every lane launchd started
       was killed when this job exited, 10-30s later, inside its first backoff.
       That is the 27h outage: 163 relaunches, each logging exactly ONE
       `(fail 1)` line, at this file's 600s cadence rather than the lane's 30s
       backoff. Reproduced in spikes/H179_generation_death/pgroup.sh.

       THE REAL FIX IS IN run_loop.sh v11 (`set -m` around the detach, so a lane
       gets its own process group whatever starts it). This key is defence in
       depth for anything else this job spawns, and it CHANGES NOTHING UNTIL A
       HUMAN RE-INSTALLS: the loaded copy lives in ~/Library/LaunchAgents, which
       is outside the workspace (§10). Logged in HUMAN_NEEDED.md. -->
  <key>AbandonProcessGroup</key>
  <true/>

  <key>StandardOutPath</key>
  <string>/Users/victorianikolenko/kingfisher/bringup.log</string>
  <key>StandardErrorPath</key>
  <string>/Users/victorianikolenko/kingfisher/bringup.log</string>
</dict>
</plist>
